AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) empowers Large Language Models (LLMs) by grounding them in external knowledge. Knowledge graphs (KGs), which capture vast amounts of facts in a structured format, offer a reliable source for RAG. However, existing methods struggle with complex, multi-hop questions, often either over-relying on the LLM’s fallible sequential reasoning or failing to generate verifiable, structured evidence from KGs. In this paper, we propose Path-Retriever, a novel two-stage framework that decouples contextual representation learning from explicit, verifiable reasoning. In the first stage, we train an instruction-guided Graph Neural Network (GNN) not as a direct answer predictor, but as a powerful feature extractor. This pre-trained GNN learns to produce structure-aware, reasoning-oriented embeddings for entities and relations tailored to a specific question context. In the second stage, we introduce an LLM-Guided Interactive Beam Search (IGBS) algorithm. This algorithm performs an explicit path exploration on the KG, using the rich embeddings from our pre-trained GNN as a powerful heuristic scoring function. Crucially, after each expansion step, the most promising candidate paths are evaluated by LLM, which acts as a reasoning "strategist" to dynamically prune the search space and identify correct answers early. This interactive process synergizes the structural awareness of the GNN with the logical inference capabilities of the LLM. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our framework significantly outperforms existing state-of-the-art models in two widely used KGQA benchmarks (WebQSP and CWQ). Our results validate the efficacy of combining deep contextual graph representations with LLM-guided interactive search for solving complex, multi-hop questions.
