AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is emerging as a key tool to strengthen maternal health through predictive models, remote monitoring, and decision-support systems. This work presents the design and evaluation of a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)-based Question Answering (QA) pipeline applied to clinical content on pregnancy. The system integrates preprocessing, semantic segmentation, and embedding generation using biomedical RoBERTa and multilingual SBERT models, with vector indexing in ChromaDB for efficient retrieval. A Colombian official guideline on maternal health served as the knowledge base, processed into semantically coherent fragments to enhance contextual retrieval. Experimental evaluation with hybrid strategies-combining semantic and keyword-based search with Cross-Encoder reranking-achieved a hit@5 accuracy of $\mathbf{9 0 \%}$, demonstrating the pipeline's capacity to deliver clinically grounded answers. Results highlight the relevance of adapting AI systems to Spanish-language and low-resource contexts, addressing challenges such as algorithmic transparency, data representativeness, and clinical validation. The proposed approach contributes to more equitable, accurate, and explainable maternal healthcare support systems.
