AB  - With the rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs), Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a promising technique to enhance their capabilities. By integrating a retrieval mechanism into traditional generative models, RAG dynamically fetches relevant information from external knowledge bases, thereby improving the accuracy and contextual relevance of generated outputs. This paper delves into the foundational principles and framework of RAG, examining the retrieval-generation process and key modules such as loading, indexing, storage, querying, and evaluation. It also discusses critical technologies underpinning RAG systems, including data partitioning, retrieval algorithms, and generation processes, while presenting several typical implementation frameworks. Finally, we explore potential applications of RAG across various domains and outline future research directions, such as enhancing retrieval efficiency, enabling dynamic knowledge updates, optimizing performance in low-resource environments, and more. Overall, as technology continues to advance, RAG is poised to play an increasingly significant role in practical applications.
