AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) hold significant potential in the medical domain but are hindered by the “hallucination” phenomenon, where generated content may be plausible but factually incorrect. Retrieval-Augmented Generation has emerged as an effective paradigm to mitigate this by integrating external knowledge, and gradually migrates to storing and retrieving relevant information within graph structure. Currently, most Graph RAG methods in medical domain face critical limitations such as semantic poverty of the graph and the fragility of the retrieval process. We propose IRG-RAG, an Iterative Reflective Graph Retrieval-Augmented Generation framework tailored for Chinese medical question-answering, focuses on addressing these two key challenges. Our framework introduces a knowledge-enhanced graph construction method, enriching edges with contextual descriptions to endow the graph with richer semantic information. Additionally, we propose an iterative reflective retrieval strategy, which dynamically travels on the graph to retrieve relevant information, backtracks from ineffective paths to reduce noise, and incorporates negative feedback to guide subsequent explorations, mimicking humanlike problem-solving. Experiments on CMExam and CMB-Exam datasets demonstrate that our method significantly enhances the reliability of the model's answer, offering a robust solution to hallucination in domain-specific applications. Codes and data will be available at https://github.com/Damon1Zhang/IRG-RAG.
ER  - 

