AB  - Precise medical question-and-answer (QA) systems are essential for clinical decision-making, medical education, and patient care because they provide quick and precise medical information. With immense progress made, high accuracy in medical QA continues to be challenging due to biomedical information complexity and specialty. To solve these issues, this study systematically investigates other state-of-the-art Large Language Model (LLM) prompting strategies as well as retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) methods. The main objective of this paper is to thoroughly investigate and contrast the performance of several methods on the MedMCQA dataset-a well-known medical QA benchmark having about $\mathbf{4, 1 0 0}$ multiple-choice questions on diverse medical topics-such as zero-shot prompting, Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting, TF-IDF with XGBoost baseline, fine-tuned PubMed-BERT, and three RAG variants (Naive RAG, Query Expansion, and Answer Expansion). Our results show significant performance differences between methods. The standard TF-IDF+XGBoost baseline achieved low accuracy $(32.07 \%)$, while fine-tuned PubMedBERT boosted to $\mathbf{4 4. 0 \%}$. LLM-based prompting yielded significant improvements, particularly for OpenAI's GPT-4 in a zero-shot setup $(80.13 \%)$. But RAG methods significantly surpassed all other methodology, Answer Expansion-based RAG achieving the highest accuracy of about 85%. This research highlights the high promise of retrieval-enhanced methods in medical QA, calling for broader adoption and continued research on RAG frameworks, specifically those incorporating Answer Expansion, in clinical informatics applications.
ER  - 

