AB  - Domain-specific question-answering (QA) systems demand accurate and reliable responses, yet Large Language Models (LLMs) are prone to generating hallucinated answers in the absence of domain knowledge. In this pilot study, we present a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)-based hybrid QA system for Turkish hotel information and evaluate it on a dataset representing Antalya-region hotels. The proposed system integrates dense retrieval, BM25-based lexical retrieval, reciprocal rank fusion (RRF), and cross-encoder reranking with locally deployed LLM inference. A total of 239 hotel records are collected, a controlled comparative analysis of chunking strategies and embedding models is conducted, and four local LLMs are evaluated in both standalone and RAG-augmented configurations. Experimental results demonstrate that the combination of Section-Based chunking, multilingual embeddings, and hybrid retrieval yields optimal performance with 32.6% MRR improvement, 82.6% F1 improvement over the LLM-only baseline, and effectively minimizes hallucination occurrences within the evaluated samples. These findings underscore the critical role of retrieval pipeline design and document segmentation in domain-specific Turkish QA systems.
ER  - 

