AB  - The Web is a vast and intricate network of interconnected information sources. While traditional Web search engines have long enabled users to search through billions of linked pages using keyword matching, the emergence of Large Language Models (LLMs) marks a fundamental shift in how we search for and process online information. Organizations are increasingly adopting LLM-based solutions, particularly retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), to automate information extraction from Web documents, going beyond simple keyword matching to understand semantic relationships between content. However, while RAG excels at reasoning within a single context window, it faces significant challenges in multi-hop inference, particularly in Web search scenarios where information must be gathered and processed from different sources. Graph-augmented RAGs address these limitations by leveraging graph-structured data to capture relationships between documents and enable more context-aware responses. This work investigates whether LLMs, enhanced with Web document-level graph-augmented RAG, can better automate the process of navigating and synthesizing information from complex Web documents. Our approach exploits both the textual content of Web documents and their hyperlink structure to create a comprehensive framework for Web-based information navigation and synthesis. We evaluate our approach on benchmark a multi-hop question-answering datasets, and demonstrate measurable improvements in the retrieval performance of information synthesis when compared to baseline approaches. The code and prompt templates are available at https://github.com/mehrshaad/Multi-hop-WebRAG.
