AB  - In recent years, large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong capabilities in natural language understanding and text generation. However, applying them directly to question-answering tasks in the field of power electronics still poses certain challenges, primarily because these models are prone to hallucinations and lack sufficient domain-specific knowledge. Questions in the power electronics field typically involve converter topologies, power devices, control strategies, and formula-based reasoning, thus requiring generated answers to be both accurate and traceable.In order to solve these problems, the paper suggests the implementation of a hybrid Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) question-answering system in the power electronics field. The system builds a knowledge base based on the power electronics relevant documents and saves text embeddings in ChromaDB. In the inference stage, it integrates both semantic vector retrieval and BM25 keyword retrieval through Reciprocal Ranking Fusion (RRF), and thus considers the semantic similarity and the technical term match simultaneously. Afterward, the retrieved Top-K context is incorporated in the prompt and entered into the DeepSeek model to produce an answer.The paper uses the RAGAS model to evaluate the system. The experimental findings prove the hybrid retrieval method to be with a Faithfulness score of 0.9764, an Answer Relevancy score of 0.8920 and a Context Precision score of 0.9554. These findings prove that the suggested approach increases the contextually-based nature of the produced answers and makes LLMS more reliable in question and answer (Q&A) tasks in power electronics.
