AB  - This study evaluates the effectiveness of opensource large language models (LLMs) for answering questions about the Philippine Labor Code using a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) framework. Three instruction-tuned models-LLaMA 3.1 8B, Qwen2.5 7B, and Gemma 2 9B-were deployed within an identical hybrid retrieval pipeline combining BM25, dense embeddings, reciprocal rank fusion, and cross-encoder reranking. Evaluation was conducted on 30 benchmark queries (27 in-scope and 3 out-of-scope) using metrics including semantic similarity, faithfulness, answer relevancy, citation accuracy, LLM-as-a-Judge scoring, and response latency. Retrieval performance was identical across models (Recall $\approx 0.99$; Precision $\approx 0.31$), while differences emerged in generation quality. Gemma 29 B achieved the highest semantic similarity (0.859), citation accuracy (0.935), and judge score (0.733), indicating stronger legal grounding. Qwen2.5 7B showed the fastest responses (8.89 s) and highest faithfulness, while LLaMA 3.1 8B achieved slightly higher answer relevancy. All models correctly refused 100% of out-ofscope queries. The results demonstrate that RAG-enhanced open-source LLMs can provide reliable and accessible legal information systems for Philippine labor law assistance.
