AB  - Hazard and Operability (HAZOP) analysis is a critical methodology for risk assessment in the chemical process industry. However, the valuable engineering knowledge generated from these analyses often resides in static, semi-structured documents, hindering effective retrieval and reuse. Standard Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) approaches struggle in this domain due to semantic mismatches during retrieval and the lack of structured reasoning in generation. To address these challenges, this study introduces a novel framework for intelligent question answering on HAZOP reports, named the Domain-Adaptive and Reranking-Enhanced Retrieval-Augmented Generation (DR-RAG) framework. The framework features three key innovations: a domain-adaptive retriever fine-tuned via contrastive learning with hard negatives to enhance semantic discrimination; a dual-path retrieval strategy combined with cross-encoder reranking to ensure high precision across heterogeneous data; and a JSON Schema-constrained generation module that mandates the Large Language Model to produce structured outputs aligned with HAZOP causal logic. Experimental results on a real-world industrial dataset demonstrate that DR-RAG significantly outperforms baselines, improving context precision from 65.0 percent to 96.7 percent and elevating end-to-end answer accuracy from 1.25 to 1.90 on a 0 to 2 scale. This work offers a robust and scalable solution for automating knowledge engineering in industrial safety.
ER  - 

