AB  - Mine safety demands rapid access to accurate and reliable knowledge to reduce accidents and support critical decision-making. Existing question-answering (QA) systems struggle in this domain due to specialized terminology, complex multi-part queries, and the risk of hallucinated responses from general-purpose models. To address these challenges, we formalize the Mine Safety Question Answering task, which focuses on delivering precise and context-aware answers for safety-critical mining scenarios. We propose a domain-adapted large language model framework that combines parameter-efficient fine-tuning via LoRA with a multi-expert knowledge fusion mechanism, enabling effective injection of mining-specific expertise while controlling computational cost. To support evaluation, we construct a MineSafetyQA dataset and additionally adopt a Construction Safety QA benchmark for cross-domain validation. Experimental results demonstrate that our approach consistently outperforms state-of-the-art baselines by 2–3% in accuracy and F1 score across datasets, achieving 88.0% accuracy on MineSafetyQA. Ablation studies further verify the contributions of domain adaptation and knowledge fusion, with robust performance maintained even when trained on only 50% of the data. These results highlight the effectiveness of specialized LLM adaptation for safety-critical QA and suggest broader applicability to other high-risk industrial domains.
ER  - 

