AB  - There are complex rules and regulations in the governance of the deep sea, which include the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS), regulations by the International Seabed Authority, and Environmental Impact Assessments. The rules and regulations contain a huge volume of information, which is hard to analyze with the help of traditional search algorithms. In this paper, a new model has been proposed, which is termed “Retrieval Augmented Generation” and is suitable to meet the requirements of the domain of rules and regulations in the governance of the deep sea. The model uses different forms of chunking. The performance of the system can be measured using several parameters, including Precision@K, Recall@K, Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR), and Hit Rate, in the context of the retrieval system. On the other hand, the quality of the generated text can be measured using several parameters, including ROUGE-L, BLEU, BERTScore, faithfulness, and answer relevance. Based on the experiments performed on the system using datasets created from ISA legal documents and benchmark question answering datasets, it is evident that the optimization of chunking and embedding improves the accuracy of the system's performance, reducing hallucinations in the response texts. The proposed framework for deep question answering can be advantageous in decision-making processes, as it provides transparency using source grounding and citations.
ER  - 

