AB  - Nowadays large language models (LLMs) are widely used as an assistant for natural language generation and editorial tasks. There are several free of charge and paid chatbots available online, but their usage poses an information security threat connected with confidentiality. In order to mitigate this issue, a deployment of open-source LLMs to a secure environment is used. Given the high cost of GPUs, LLMs with a minimal number of parameters should be considered. But such models are unable to execute the task of domain-specific text generation. There are several ways to solve this issue: train LLM on a domain-specific corpus of data with the help of the parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) approach or utilize the retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) technique. In the first case, a large dataset is needed, so if the dataset is relatively small, the RAG technique is the only choice. In the paper, the method that extends capabilities of the RAG approach is described along with the chatbot that uses this method for generation of domain-specific document templates. Testing was performed on the dataset of depersonalized magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) reports.
