AB  - Knowledge-Based Visual Question Answering (KB- VQA) extends beyond visual perception by requiring external knowledge and reasoning, making it particularly susceptible to hallucinations in Large Language Model (LLM)–driven systems. These hallucinated responses that may be logically inconsistent, primarily due to the absence of explicit answer validation mechanisms and lack of contextual prompting methods. CRAG- VQA, a Contextual RAG framework for KB-VQA with Hallucination Mitigation is proposed to resolve the above issues. This work handles knowledge-intensive visual queries that require reasoning beyond directly observable image features. CRAG-VQA dynamically retrieves and integrates multimodal and knowledge- grounded context—including visual evidence, semantic attributes, and external knowledge sources—conditioned on the input image and query. The proposed framework employs contextual prompting to explicitly ground LLM reasoning in retrieved evidence, while a consistency-based verification mechanism aligns generated answers with both visual cues and knowledge context. By decoupling knowledge retrieval from answer generation and enforcing contextual alignment, CRAG-VQA effectively reduces unsupported inferences and factual drift. The proposed system minimizes Hallucination using Consistency Checking to emphasize visually supported information while minimizing reasoning errors and dependence on unconstrained parametric knowledge. Experimental evaluation on standard VQA benchmarks confirms notable gains in accuracy for multimodal reasoning in KB-VQA.
