AB  - Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) systems address the well-known limitations of large language models (LLMs),namely knowledge staleness and factual hallucination, by grounding generation in dynamically retrieved documents. Single-modality dense retrieval, however, systematically fails on queries requiring precise keyword matching, such as error codes, product identifiers, or regulatory references. This paper presents a modular, reproducible hybrid RAG pipeline that combines FAISS-based dense retrieval with BM25 lexical search and a Cross-Encoder reranking step, orchestrated through an explicit LangGraph state machine. Using efficient CPU-friendly models (all-MiniLM-L6-v2 for embeddings and ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 for reranking), the system demonstrates that architectural sophistication can substitute for raw model scale. Evaluation on a 2,000-sample subset of the HotpotQA distractor benchmark shows that the full pipeline raises nDCG from 0.652 (vector-only baseline) to 0.790 (Hybrid+Rerank), a statistically significant improvement (t = −24.29, p < 10−114). Hallucination rate, assessed with an automated LLM judge, drops from 0.20 to 0.40 grounding score (higher is better). A complete reproducibility package, including source code, data injection scripts, and cached indices,is provided so that all reported results can be independently verified with a single command.
ER  - 

