AB  - Legal question answering requires both strong evidence recall and faithful multi-hop reasoning, yet vanilla RAG pipelines often struggle to organize cross-article statutory structure and may produce high-risk errors. We propose a lightweight Structure-Adapter that augments a text-retrieval backbone with a parallel knowledge-graph branch: the system retrieves relevant subgraphs and high-confidence multi-hop paths, encodes each path into compact structural and textual representations, and injects the fused representations as continuous "knowledge soft prompts" into the LLM ’ s embedding space at predefined positions in the hard prompt. To keep training efficient, we freeze the LLM backbone and optimize only the path-level adapter and a small projector, enabling low-cost adaptation while preserving the model’s generation ability. We evaluate on a 488-sample benchmark randomly drawn from the LAW-GPT 92k dataset with reference-grounded supervision, using standard text-overlap metrics and a high-risk error rate (Badcase_Rate). Results show that the proposed method achieves the best overall performance (F1 0.5246, ACC 0.4068) and the lowest Badcase_Rate (0.0082), outperforming strong RAG baselines including RQ-RAG.
