AB  - The rapid growth of digital documents in academic, legal, and professional spaces has increased the demand for more intelligent systems which enable efficient knowledge retrieval. Portable Document Format (PDF) files, which although widely used, present challenges due to their static nature and lack of desirable semantic search capabilities. This paper highlights a system for conversational interaction with multiple PDFs using a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) approach. This proposed framework extracts and preprocesses document text, encodes it into vector embeddings, and stores it in a vector database for efficient retrieval. When a query is issued by the user, the system retrieves relevant document segments across multiple PDFs and integrates them into a large language model (LLM) prompt to provide contextually grounded responses. Such architecture facilitates multi document management through metadata tagging and conversational memory so that the dialogue can be smooth and that it can also transcend other sources. Research has shown that this method enhances the accuracy and relevance of the responses as opposed to the search using keywords as well as question answering using single document.This system has applications in research, education, law, and enterprise settings, where users require interactive and semantically rich access to large collections of documents.
