AB  - Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has emerged as a promising approach for enhancing large language models’ performance in domain-specific tasks. However, traditional RAG systems face challenges in legal question answering due to the complexity and specificity of legal texts. This paper proposes an enhanced RAG framework that integrates three key improvements: LLM-based intelligent chunking, advanced Qwen3-Embedding 8B model for semantic representation, and a hybrid retrieval strategy combining semantic matching, BM25 keyword search, and knowledge graph reasoning with learning-to-rank fusion. We evaluate our approach on the Chinese LawBench dataset using two state-of-the-art language models. Experimental results demonstrate that our enhanced RAG system achieves accuracy improvements of up to $\mathbf{4. 2 \%}$ for knowledge-based questions and $\mathbf{8. 0 \%}$ for case analysis tasks compared to baseline RAG implementations, with the best performance reaching $80.8 \%$ and $78.8 \%$ accuracy respectively.
