AB  - We introduce a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)-based system that is used to answer domainspecific questions (question answering (QA)). It combines web scraping, semantic embeddings, vector similarity search, and large language model (LLM) generation. The suggested architecture is an automatized end-to-end pipeline of domain-relevant online data acquisition, preprocessing into structured textual fragments, and embedding the textual fragments with high-dimensional embedding models to facilitate efficient retrieval via a FAISS vector index. After retrieving the relevant documents a Groqpowered LLM synthesizes these data to generate correct and context-sensitive answers based on the source content. In addition to outlining the system workflow, this paper will give a line by line analysis of each module such as the scraping engine, the chunking and embedding strategies, the retrieval mechanisms, and the prompt-engineering factors applied in the process of generating an answer. Experimental analyses reveal that the system is capable of addressing dynamic online content, high-retrieval accuracy and also factual accuracy is significantly enhanced over non-RAG baselines. The proposed approach emphasizes modularity, real-time adaptability, and multi-metric evaluation, making it suitable for practical deployment in dynamic knowledge environments.
ER  - 

